1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,130
Three supplements.

2
00:00:01,640 --> 00:00:03,890
Same letter on the front, more or less.

3
00:00:04,180 --> 00:00:07,570
In a quiet year, one of them costs
you twenty-nine hundred dollars…

4
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:10,430
and one of them costs twelve hundred.

5
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:14,650
In a bad year, the cheap one
becomes the expensive one.

6
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:18,690
This is Run the Number.

7
00:00:19,060 --> 00:00:25,850
One person, one year, three Medicare supplements:
Plan G, Plan N, and the high-deductible

8
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:27,110
version of G.

9
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:32,070
By the end you'll know what each one costs
when nothing happens, what each one costs

10
00:00:32,120 --> 00:00:36,590
when something does, and the one number
in your own paperwork that decides which

11
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:37,890
letter fits you.

12
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:40,310
This is education, not advice.

13
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:42,310
Nobody here sells insurance.

14
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:47,750
Start with what the three letters actually
are, because the names hide it.

15
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:53,790
Plan G pays everything Medicare leaves behind
except one thing: the Part B deductible.

16
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:56,970
Two hundred eighty-three dollars
for twenty twenty-six.

17
00:00:57,250 --> 00:00:59,230
After that, no bills.

18
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:02,530
Plan N pays the same big things…

19
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:05,110
the hospital deductible,
the twenty percent…

20
00:01:05,250 --> 00:01:07,550
but it hands you two small bills.

21
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:10,030
Up to twenty dollars for an office visit.

22
00:01:10,220 --> 00:01:14,510
Up to fifty dollars for an emergency room
visit that doesn't turn into an admission.

23
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:18,170
And one thing it doesn't cover
at all, which we'll get to.

24
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:22,530
High-deductible G is Plan
G with a front door.

25
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:27,070
You pay the Medicare bills yourself until
they reach the deductible for the year.

26
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:32,140
For twenty twenty-seven, Medicare set that
number in October: three thousand fifty

27
00:01:32,190 --> 00:01:32,990
dollars.

28
00:01:33,380 --> 00:01:35,390
After that, it's Plan G.

29
00:01:36,420 --> 00:01:37,350
Now the premiums.

30
00:01:38,020 --> 00:01:42,290
These are national averages from quotes on
Medicare dot gov for twenty twenty-six,

31
00:01:42,340 --> 00:01:43,550
at sixty-five.

32
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:47,130
Yours will be different, and that's
the point of the exercise.

33
00:01:48,220 --> 00:01:50,910
Plan G: about two hundred twenty a month.

34
00:01:51,380 --> 00:01:53,770
Plan N: about one seventy.

35
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:56,490
High-deductible G: about sixty.

36
00:01:57,040 --> 00:02:01,920
So before anyone gets sick, the year starts
at two thousand six hundred forty…

37
00:02:01,970 --> 00:02:03,350
two thousand fifty-two…

38
00:02:03,740 --> 00:02:05,430
and seven hundred thirty-two.

39
00:02:05,980 --> 00:02:07,410
Hold those three numbers.

40
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:09,870
Everything else is what the year adds.

41
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:12,070
How to check yours.

42
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:16,570
Your renewal letter, or the quote in front
of you, has the monthly premium on the

43
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:17,570
first page.

44
00:02:18,040 --> 00:02:19,210
Multiply by twelve.

45
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:22,570
That's the number you pay
even in a perfect year.

46
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:25,030
Now the quiet year.

47
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:28,830
Six doctor visits, some
blood work, no hospital.

48
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:32,390
Say fifteen hundred dollars
of Medicare-approved bills.

49
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:36,330
Plan G: the premium, plus
the Part B deductible.

50
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:38,430
Two thousand nine hundred twenty-three.

51
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:44,050
Plan N: the premium, the deductible,
and six visits at twenty dollars.

52
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:46,750
Two thousand four hundred fifty-five.

53
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:51,140
High-deductible G: the premium, plus what
Medicare leaves you on fifteen hundred

54
00:02:51,190 --> 00:02:52,490
dollars of bills…

55
00:02:52,820 --> 00:02:55,310
the deductible and twenty
percent of the rest.

56
00:02:55,540 --> 00:02:57,110
About five hundred twenty-six.

57
00:02:57,460 --> 00:02:59,290
Well under the three thousand fifty.

58
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:01,710
The year comes to twelve
hundred fifty-eight.

59
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:06,050
Quiet year: the high deductible
wins by sixteen hundred dollars.

60
00:03:06,820 --> 00:03:09,630
Plan N beats Plan G by
almost five hundred.

61
00:03:10,160 --> 00:03:11,450
Now the bad year.

62
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:14,450
One emergency room visit in the
spring that sends you home.

63
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:16,790
One hospital stay in the fall.

64
00:03:17,440 --> 00:03:19,390
Fourteen office visits across the year.

65
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:22,670
Twelve thousand dollars of Medicare-approved
doctor bills.

66
00:03:23,140 --> 00:03:24,450
Plan G doesn't move.

67
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:26,530
Two thousand nine hundred twenty-three.

68
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:29,910
That's the whole product:
the number doesn't move.

69
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:36,170
Plan N: fourteen visits at twenty, the fifty-dollar
emergency room copay, the deductible.

70
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:38,050
Two thousand six hundred sixty-five.

71
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:40,050
Still under G…

72
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:42,150
and here's the thing nobody expects.

73
00:03:42,340 --> 00:03:47,150
For Plan N to cost more than Plan G at these
premiums, you'd need about twenty-nine

74
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:49,030
office visits in one year.

75
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:53,210
The forty-nine-dollar-a-month gap
buys a lot of twenty-dollar copays.

76
00:03:53,420 --> 00:03:56,350
Except for the one thing
Plan N doesn't cover.

77
00:03:56,600 --> 00:03:58,330
Part B excess charges.

78
00:03:58,620 --> 00:04:02,610
A doctor who doesn't accept Medicare's price
can bill up to fifteen percent above

79
00:04:02,660 --> 00:04:05,210
it, and Plan N leaves that to you.

80
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:06,850
Plan G pays it.

81
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:11,070
Ninety-eight percent of doctors who take
Medicare accept its price, and some states

82
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:12,650
ban the charge outright…

83
00:04:12,880 --> 00:04:16,670
but if the specialist you want is in the
other two percent, that's the bill that

84
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:17,790
finds you.

85
00:04:17,840 --> 00:04:22,850
High-deductible G in the bad year: the hospital
deductible, the Part B deductible,

86
00:04:23,020 --> 00:04:24,770
twenty percent of the doctor bills.

87
00:04:24,940 --> 00:04:27,790
That's over four thousand
dollars of Medicare bills…

88
00:04:27,900 --> 00:04:30,630
so you pay the three thousand
fifty and stop.

89
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:31,770
Plus the premium.

90
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:33,430
Three thousand seven hundred eighty-two.

91
00:04:34,280 --> 00:04:38,610
So the letter that saved you sixteen hundred
in the quiet year costs you eight hundred

92
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:40,430
sixty more in the bad one.

93
00:04:40,740 --> 00:04:41,550
Same person.

94
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:42,810
Same three letters.

95
00:04:42,880 --> 00:04:44,070
The order flips.

96
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:48,070
What does that do to the
money over ten years?

97
00:04:48,420 --> 00:04:52,930
Hold the premiums flat, just to compare, and
give the decade two bad years and eight

98
00:04:52,980 --> 00:04:54,050
quiet ones.

99
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:57,090
Plan G: about twenty-nine thousand.

100
00:04:57,280 --> 00:04:59,660
Plan N: about twenty-five thousand.

101
00:04:59,710 --> 00:05:03,430
High-deductible G: about seventeen
thousand six hundred.

102
00:05:03,940 --> 00:05:08,970
The gap between G and the high deductible
is eleven and a half thousand dollars…

103
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:14,230
nearly a full year of a thousand-dollar-a-month
draw from savings, in exchange for

104
00:05:14,280 --> 00:05:18,050
being able to write a three-thousand-dollar
check in the years that go wrong.

105
00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:21,310
That's the real question, and
it isn't about the letters.

106
00:05:21,740 --> 00:05:25,470
Can you absorb three thousand fifty
in a bad year without it hurting?

107
00:05:25,780 --> 00:05:27,510
Then the cheap premium is cheap.

108
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:31,810
If that check would come out of money you
can't replace, the predictable number is

109
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:33,030
worth what it costs.

110
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:34,990
How to check yours.

111
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:36,350
Three lines.

112
00:05:36,820 --> 00:05:38,630
Your premium, times twelve.

113
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:43,610
Your Medicare summary notice from last year:
add up the doctor visits, and that's

114
00:05:43,660 --> 00:05:45,850
your copay exposure on Plan N.

115
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:50,670
And for the high deductible, ask one question:
what did Medicare leave me to pay

116
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:53,030
last year, before any supplement?

117
00:05:53,360 --> 00:05:55,210
That figure is on the same notice.

118
00:05:55,460 --> 00:05:59,230
If it's under three thousand, the high
deductible would have won last year.

119
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:04,970
A fee-only fiduciary or a CPA can run your
own numbers, and the State Health Insurance

120
00:06:05,020 --> 00:06:08,510
Assistance Program will compare the
three letters with you for free.

121
00:06:08,860 --> 00:06:10,890
New numbers every Tuesday and Friday.

122
00:06:11,640 --> 00:06:13,370
Subscribe so the next one finds you.

123
00:06:14,320 --> 00:06:16,810
Will your money last as long as you do?
